{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"resident-loveydovey-grub","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\n\nThe header surface is operator-facing and security-sensitive, and its value grammar\n(`${env:...}`, `${run:...}`) is rebar-specific. `docs/config.md` and `docs/llm-framework.md`\ndocument every other LLM knob, including the `LANGFUSE_*` sink at\n`docs/llm-framework.md:433`; without an entry an operator cannot discover the feature, and\nwithout security guidance they are likely to place a gateway credential in a committed file.\n\n## Approach\n\nDocument the knob where the other LLM knobs already live, rather than in a new page: an\noperator looking for LLM configuration reads these two files.\n\nAdd to `docs/config.md` the `llm.headers` key, its three resolution layers and precedence,\nthe `${env:...}` and `${run:...}` grammar with the closed `${run:...}` vocabulary, and the\nrejected header names.\n\nAdd to `docs/llm-framework.md` a gateway-observability recipe showing at least two different\ngateways, so no vendor reads as the supported one. Include the caveat that nginx strips\nheader names containing underscores by default, which is why a gateway's underscore-form\nheader may silently never arrive.\n\nState the security guidance explicitly: values may reference environment variables so a\ncredential need not be committed; authentication header names are rejected because caller\nheaders are merged last by the vendor SDKs and would displace the real credential; and\nheader values are recorded nowhere, only names.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `docs/config.md` — the `llm.headers` key, layers, grammar and rejected names.\n- `docs/llm-framework.md` — the gateway recipe, the nginx caveat, the security guidance.\n\n## Testing\n\nNo `## Testing` section beyond this note: the change adds no executable behavior. Its\nacceptance is verified by the documentation-parity assertions below, which are grep-shaped\nchecks over the committed files rather than behavioral tests.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `docs/config.md` documents `llm.headers`, its three layers with precedence, and the\n      closed `${run:...}` vocabulary; checked by reading the rendered section and by a grep\n      asserting each of `trace_id`, `ticket_id`, `operation`, `run_id` appears in it.\n- [ ] `docs/config.md` lists every rejected header name; checked by a grep asserting all five\n      of `authorization`, `x-api-key`, `cookie`, `host`, `proxy-authorization` appear.\n- [ ] `docs/llm-framework.md` shows the recipe for at least two distinct gateways; checked by\n      a grep asserting two different gateway header prefixes appear in the section.\n- [ ] The nginx underscore-stripping caveat is stated; checked by a grep for \"underscore\" in\n      the new section.\n- [ ] The security guidance states that values may use `${env:...}`, that authentication\n      header names are rejected, and that values are never recorded; checked by reading the\n      rendered section against those three points.","id":"98b6-efa8-bf15-4760","parent_id":"7e9e-07f7-8fe1-4e38","priority":3,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"task","title":"Document the llm.headers gateway-observability surface and its security rules"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787705286257603001,"uuid":"6bb70e69-ee2c-4f80-9274-8724492164ce"}